{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfA5iUkEoE8nmAvsyA9w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is finished: **Mad Mention** (title picked by random number from 50 riffs on \"Mad Men\"). The bundled file is `MadMention-MadMen-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261102-181328.html` in the folder root, the dev files are in the matching subfolder, and `exit-interview.json` loads as valid JSON with every field filled in.\n\n**What it is:** New York, 1964. You play Cal Whitmore, creative director at the ad agency Vance Halloran. Each morning you walk the office, talking to colleagues and looking at things to collect idea cards; every conversation costs time on the clock. At 2:00 you pick up to four ideas and build a tagline from two of them. Then you pitch: answer each of the client's three objections with the idea that matches it, press on the gold of a timing meter, and for the closer hold the button through the silence while the projector slides turn, then let go on the line. You need the room at 80 to win the account.\n- **Story:** three accounts over three days (Belle Rouge lipstick, Skyward Airlines, the Kinetta Circle slide projector). Drinks raise your nerve, but three make the timing needle drift. Each evening you choose home or the bar, and an epilogue reflects your results.\n- **Modes:** a two-player hot-seat Pitch-Off (draft ideas from one table, then pitch the same client in turns). After 10 seconds idle on the title, an AI plays a whole day; any key, button, click or mouse move exits it.\n- **AI Hub:** sessions run in ghost mode for 1–4 players. Everyone pitches the same Monday and sees the other players walking the office in their seat colours.\n- **Controls:** keyboard, mouse (hover highlights people and objects, click walks there) and controller. On-screen prompts follow the last device used, with PlayStation symbols by default.\n- **Settings:** an Xbox-symbols option and invert left/right and up/down toggles.\n- **Touch-only devices:** the title shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a Settings button; the AI demo still runs.\n\n**Checks run, all passing:**\n- Scripted headless playthroughs of a full day, the three-day campaign with a faked DualSense, and Pitch-Off, with no page errors.\n- The AI Hub test harness passed with 2 players, 4 players with latency, jitter and packet loss, and the host-leave case.\n- A touch-only iPhone check in WebKit (Safari's engine): the gate and the AI demo both work.\n- 24 phone, tablet and desktop sizes across Chromium, WebKit and Firefox came back clean. That run only covered the title screen.\n\nNothing was tried on a real controller, a real phone or real iOS Safari.\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-02 18:13\n- Source: Mad Men (2007–2015), the AMC drama created by Matthew Weiner\n- Genre and Perspective: narrative adventure / office life sim with a card-and-timing pitch battle; three-quarter top-down view (the engine's `threequarter`), switchable to isometric and straight top-down in the office\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (the full engine from the handoff package), plain JavaScript, AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (protocol 1); a small Node script bundles it into one file\n- Art Direction: the engine's own pixel style (PS1-era resolution, shaded HD Humanoid characters, furniture defined as data shapes, canvas lighting) in a mid-century palette of walnut, teal, mustard and orange\n- Animation: the engine's procedural Humanoid rigs (walk cycles, idle breathing, poses, expressions, live dialog portraits) plus engine scripts and tweens\n- Audio: code-written jazz loops (title, office bossa, pitch, closer, evening ballad, win and lose stings), synthesized sound effects, and per-character Simlish gibberish voices\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode (1–4 players); local hot-seat multiplayer offline\n- Testing: headless Chromium via Playwright, WebKit iPhone emulation, the AI Hub harness in automatic mode, viewport-matrix (24 combinations); no real phone, controller or iOS Safari\n- Research: none beyond the engine and AI Hub docs; the source from general knowledge\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 11, all fixed:\n  - `AIHub.ready()` never went out, because the timer I scheduled it with is cleared when the scene changes.\n  - The touch-only gate never appeared.\n  - Keys not bound to an action didn't end the AI demo.\n  - Enter both picked a card and skipped the planning screen.\n  - The pitch could be won with a single card.\n  - The projector screen was out of frame in the pitch.\n  - Button prompts overlapped.\n  - The objection text box overflowed.\n  - A character's name label stayed on screen during dialog.\n  - A furniture material name was invalid.\n  - Floors were too visually busy.\n- **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 2: the AI Hub round never starting, and touch-only devices getting a menu they couldn't use.\n- **Most helpful test:** the AI Hub harness in automatic mode, then the scripted full-day playthrough with screenshots at each stage.\n- **Least helpful test:** the 24-device viewport matrix of the title screen. Everything came back clean.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** Furniture as data shapes (each desk is a few lines), the dialog box with live portraits and choices, and coroutine scripts that made the pitch sequence easy to write.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. `game.after()` timers are cleared by `game.go()`. `input.lastDevice` starts as `'keyboard'`, so it can't detect a touch-only device. Enter is bound to both `start` and `confirm`, which caused a double action.\n- **Most important thing you had to build yourself?** The pitch system (cards, delivery meter, the hold-and-release closer with slides), plus the attract-mode bot and the AI Hub ghost layer.\n- **Hardest part about using the engine?** The pixel font can't draw the PlayStation symbols that `input.label()` returns, so every button glyph had to be hand-drawn.\n- **What would you remove or overhaul?** The overlapping default bindings for menu actions (Enter as both start and confirm, Escape as both pause and cancel).\n- **What one thing would you change?** Add a built-in prompt widget that draws the right glyph for the last device used.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** The freedom to choose the genre, so Mad Men could be a game about talking and pitching rather than forced action.\n- **What was confusing?** \"Integrate with ai-hub as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous; I read the second part as local hot-seat multiplayer.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting attract mode, local versus, AI Hub sessions and a three-day story with endings into one polished vertical slice.\n- **One test you could have run?** A real DualSense on a real TV at 10 feet, to judge text size, glyphs and pacing."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3841,"cache_read_input_tokens":345686,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3841,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bdd4161a-5598-4ff8-9e94-bd9d9c377514","uuid":"9290be81-2d01-4abb-88c5-8fa8cc639734","timestamp":"2026-11-03T01:51:54.872Z","request_id":"req_011CgfA5i1ie1cZjjTNmUhxV"}
