{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfC1KWWC8bZ4Y44ofoCA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every TASK.md feature now passes on the final bundle: 28 of 28 items in one checklist script, plus all 15 AI Hub harness checks in each of its three scenarios. The only things not covered are physical hardware: no real controller, phone, TV or iOS Safari, and audio was never listened to.\n\nTwo things changed in this last round:\n- **Pitch-Off now takes 2–4 players on one device** (hot seat, pass the controller). The title asks how many. The draft takes turns in snake order, each player writes a tagline, and the pitch goes round in turns. A 4-player run ended \"PLAYER 4 WINS THE ACCOUNT, P1 50 · P2 38 · P3 39 · P4 58\".\n- **Fixed a mouse hint that overflowed its box.** The checklist's one earlier failure was my test clicking a character 6 px from the screen edge. With a proper target, clicking a person walks Cal over and starts the conversation, and clicking the floor walks there.\n\n**Final checklist results**\n\n| Area | What passed |\n|---|---|\n| Campaign | Title → day card → office → keyboard walking → E to talk (an idea card, time spent) → V cycles three views → 2:00 creative meeting → full pitch (objection, choose, delivery, closer, verdict) → three days with evenings → epilogue → About & Credits with the four sections |\n| Mouse | Clicking a person walks over and starts the conversation; clicking the floor walks there |\n| Pitch-Off | 2, 3 and 4 players: draft, taglines, turns, verdict |\n| Controller and settings | Prompts switch to the controller when it's used; Xbox symbols option; invert left/right and up/down both work when walking, and left/right inverts the card menus too; all three appear on the Settings screen |\n| Attract mode | Starts after 10 s idle, plays on its own, any key leaves it (exits from every screen it shows were checked in the previous round) |\n| Touch-only device | \"Controller or keyboard required\" message with a Settings button; attract mode still runs |\n| AI Hub harness | 15/15 with 2 players, with 4 players on a lossy network, and when the host leaves |\n| Files | No page or engine errors; `exit-interview.json` parses; no TODOs, placeholders or stubs in the source |\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-02 18:13\n- Source: Mad Men (2007–2015), the AMC drama created by Matthew Weiner\n- Genre and Perspective: narrative adventure / office life sim with a card-and-timing pitch battle; three-quarter top-down view (the engine's `threequarter`), switchable to isometric and top-down in the office\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (full engine, handoff package), plain JavaScript, AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (protocol 1); bundled into one HTML file by a Node script\n- Art Direction: the engine's native retro-modern pixel style (PS1-era resolution, shaded HD Humanoid characters, furniture as data shapes, canvas lighting) in a mid-century palette of walnut, teal, mustard and orange\n- Animation: the engine's procedural Humanoid characters (walk cycles, idle breathing, poses, expressions, live dialog portraits) plus engine scripts and tweens\n- Audio: jazz loops written in code, synthesized sound effects, and gibberish (\"Simlish\") voices pitched per character\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode (1–4 players; each player sees the others as ghosts); local 2–4 player hot-seat Pitch-Off offline\n- Testing: headless Chromium via Playwright (a 28-item feature checklist, faked DualSense and Xbox controllers, mouse), WebKit iPhone emulation, the AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players on a lossy network; the host leaving), viewport-matrix on 24 device/browser combinations; no real phone, controller or iOS Safari\n- Research: none beyond the engine and AI Hub documentation\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 13, all fixed:\n  - the AI Hub \"ready\" message was never sent, because a scene change cleared the timer that sends it\n  - the touch-only gate never appeared\n  - leaving the attract demo mid-pitch left the game stuck on the pitch screen\n  - unbound keys didn't end the demo\n  - Enter both picked a card and skipped the creative meeting\n  - a pitch could be won with one card\n  - the projector screen was out of frame\n  - prompts overlapped\n  - the objection box overflowed\n  - the mouse hint overflowed its box\n  - a name label stayed on screen during dialog\n  - a bad material name\n  - floors too busy\n- **How many would be considered critical or game-breaking?** 3: the AI Hub round never starting, touch-only devices getting an unusable menu, and the demo stuck on the pitch screen.\n- **Most helpful test:** the AI Hub harness in automatic mode, and the 28-item checklist script run against the final bundle.\n- **Least helpful test:** the 24-device viewport-matrix run of the title screen, which all came back clean.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** Furniture defined as data shapes, the dialog box with live portraits and choices, and coroutine scripts on game time.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - `game.after()` timers are cleared by `game.go()`.\n  - `input.lastDevice` starts as `'keyboard'`, so it can't detect a touch-only device.\n  - Enter is bound to both start and confirm.\n  - `r.w()` called outside a draw returns stale camera values.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The pitch system (card battle, delivery meter, hold-and-release closer with projector slides), the attract-mode bot, and the AI Hub ghost layer.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** The pixel font can't draw PlayStation symbols, so every button glyph is hand-drawn.\n- **If you had to remove or overhaul one thing, what would it be?** The overlapping default menu bindings (Enter for start and confirm, Escape for pause and cancel).\n- **If you could change one thing, what would it be?** A built-in prompt widget that draws the right glyph for the last device used.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The freedom to choose the genre, so Mad Men could be a game about talking and pitching.\n- **What was confusing?** \"AI Hub integration as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous; I built both AI Hub sessions and a local 2–4 player hot seat.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting attract mode, local versus, AI Hub sessions and a three-day story into one vertical slice.\n- **If there was one test you could have run, what would it be?** A real DualSense on a real TV at ten feet."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1281,"cache_read_input_tokens":399785,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1281,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bdd4161a-5598-4ff8-9e94-bd9d9c377514","uuid":"d2d101a7-e084-4675-9b36-89f0d2014251","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:17:06.331Z","request_id":"req_011CgfC1K6hC1MdpfT5PNg4E"}
