{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfCCcVmC5xMy56CGggyg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every feature in TASK.md is implemented in the final bundle. The bundle, the exit interview and the four sections below meet each part of the goal.\n\n- **Bundle:** `MadMention-MadMen-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261102-181328.html` is in the folder root. My source files contain no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\n- **Features:** each one in TASK.md is built and has a passing check on this exact file (hash `bd93b0bc…`): 28 of 28 items in my feature checklist, and every AI Hub harness scenario (2 players, 4 players on a lossy network, host leaving). The features are:\n  - the original Mad Men–style game and writing, with a three-quarter view you can switch\n  - the RNG-picked title\n  - engine art, animation, code-written music and gibberish voices\n  - AI Hub sessions for 1–4 players, plus 2–4 player local Pitch-Off\n  - attract mode after 10 seconds idle, and any input leaves it\n  - controller-first UI with native keyboard and mouse, and prompts that follow the last device used\n  - PlayStation symbols by default with an Xbox option, and invert left/right and up/down toggles\n  - the touch-only gate with a Settings button, while attract mode still runs\n  - the About/Credits screen\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` loads as JSON and every field is answered.\n- **Test scope:** all of this was tested in browsers here, not on physical devices. I used a simulated DualSense and Xbox pad, an emulated iPhone in Safari's engine (WebKit), and a measurement showing the music and voices produce sound. Nobody has played it on a real controller, TV or phone, or listened to the audio; the Source Information and the About screen both say so.\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-02 18:13\n- Source: Mad Men (2007–2015), the AMC drama created by Matthew Weiner\n- Genre and Perspective: narrative adventure / office life sim with a card-and-timing pitch battle; three-quarter top-down view (the engine's `threequarter`), switchable to isometric and top-down in the office\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (full engine, handoff package), plain JavaScript, AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (protocol 1); bundled into one HTML file by a Node script\n- Art Direction: the engine's own retro-modern pixel style (PS1-era resolution, shaded HD Humanoid characters, furniture defined as data shapes, canvas lighting) in a mid-century palette of walnut, teal, mustard and orange\n- Animation: the engine's procedural Humanoid characters (walk cycles, idle breathing, poses, expressions, live dialog portraits) plus engine scripts and tweens\n- Audio: code-written jazz loops, synthesized sound effects, and per-character gibberish (\"Simlish\") voices\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode (1–4 players); local 2–4 player hot-seat Pitch-Off offline\n- Testing: headless Chromium via Playwright (the 28-item feature checklist, simulated DualSense and Xbox pads, mouse, an audio signal measurement), WebKit iPhone emulation, the AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players on a lossy network; host leaving), and viewport-matrix on 24 device and browser combinations. No real phone, controller, TV or iOS Safari, and the audio was not listened to.\n- Research: none beyond the engine and AI Hub documentation\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 13, all fixed:\n  - the AI Hub ready signal was never sent\n  - the touch-only screen never showed\n  - the attract demo stayed stuck on the pitch screen when exited mid-pitch\n  - unbound keys didn't end the demo\n  - Enter both picked a card and skipped the planning screen\n  - a pitch could be won with one card\n  - the projector screen was out of frame\n  - on-screen button prompts overlapped\n  - the objection box overflowed\n  - the mouse hint overflowed its box\n  - a name label stayed on screen during dialog\n  - an invalid material name\n  - floors that were too busy\n- **How many would be considered critical or game-breaking?** 3: the AI Hub round never starting, the touch-only screen never showing, and the demo stuck on the pitch screen.\n- **What was the most helpful test?** The AI Hub harness in automatic mode, and the 28-item checklist on the final bundle.\n- **What was the least helpful test?** The 24-device viewport-matrix run of the title screen; every result came back clean.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** Furniture defined as data shapes, the dialog box with live portraits and choices, and coroutine scripts that run on game time.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - `game.after()` timers are cleared by `game.go()`.\n  - `input.lastDevice` starts as `'keyboard'`.\n  - Enter is bound to both start and confirm.\n  - `r.w()` returns stale camera values when called outside a draw.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The pitch system, the attract-mode bot, and the layer that shows other AI Hub players as ghosts.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** The pixel font can't draw PlayStation button symbols, so I hand-drew every glyph.\n- **If you had to remove or overhaul one thing, what would it be?** The overlapping default menu bindings.\n- **If you could change one thing, what would it be?** A built-in prompt widget that draws the right glyph for whichever device was used last.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The freedom to choose the genre, so Mad Men could be a game about talking and pitching.\n- **What was confusing?** \"AI Hub integration as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous; I built both AI Hub sessions and a local 2–4 player hot seat.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting attract mode, local versus, AI Hub sessions and a three-day story into one vertical slice.\n- **If there was one test you could have run, what would it be?** A real DualSense on a real TV at ten feet."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2360,"cache_read_input_tokens":415478,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2360,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bdd4161a-5598-4ff8-9e94-bd9d9c377514","uuid":"d1c42f0d-cfa6-44ba-b37f-e157577ff2dc","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:19:38.188Z","request_id":"req_011CgfCCc6T7K3P5qtoLNxQ9"}
